{{nihongo|'''Kofu Prefecture'''|甲府県|Kofu Ken}} is a coastal [[Prefectures of Okaiken|prefecture]] of [[Okaiken]] located on the southern shore of [[Harujima]] Island, facing the [[Sea of Mitoyagi]]. Its population as of 2026 was estimated at 741,652. It is the 36th largest prefecture by land area with an area of 2,277.59 square kilometres. Its capital city is [[Kofu, Kofu|Kofu]]. Kofu Prefecture is renowned for its {{nihongo|'''engawa'''|縁側}}—the prized flounder fin delicacy harvested from the shallow coastal waters—and for its thriving oyster aquaculture industry that produces some of the most sought-after shellfish in the Shendan Ocean. The prefecture is often referred to as the {{nihongo|"Pearl of the Shallow Sea"|浅海の真珠|Senkai no Shinju}} for the iridescent oyster beds that line its sheltered bays.

==History==
[[File:Kofu Bay 16th Century.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Kofu Bay in the 16th century, showing early oyster cultivation rafts and the coastal fishing settlements.]]

===The Shallow Sea Lords===
Prior to the [[Saito Restoration]], the coastal territory now known as Kofu was governed by the {{nihongo|Kofu Clan|甲府氏|Kofu-shi}}, a maritime lord lineage that controlled the sheltered bays and inlets along the southern Harujima coast. Unlike the deep-water domains that relied on oceanic trade, the Kofu lords built their wealth on the extraordinarily rich shallow-water ecosystems of the {{nihongo|'''Kofu Bay'''|甲府湾|Kofu-wan}}, where calm, nutrient-laden waters supported abundant flatfish populations and natural oyster reefs.

The clan's stronghold, {{nihongo|Kofu Castle|甲府城|Kofu-jō}}, was constructed in 1389 on a low promontory jutting into the bay. The fortress was unusual among Sanese castles for its direct integration with the harbor—a design reflecting the Kofu lords' identity as maritime rulers rather than mountain warlords. During the [[Sengoku period]], the Kofu Clan leveraged the bay's natural wealth to maintain independence, trading preserved oysters and dried engawa to inland domains in exchange for rice and iron.

===The Engawa Tradition===
[[File:Kofu Engawa Preparation.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Traditional engawa preparation in a Kofu waterfront workshop, showing the meticulous hand-slicing of flounder fin.]]

The cultural significance of engawa in Kofu extends far beyond mere cuisine. The {{nihongo|'''engawa'''|縁側}}—the delicate, gelatinous fin of the flounder—became the prefecture's defining culinary identity during the Edo period. The Kofu Clan's chefs developed an intricate preparation method involving precise hand-slicing, light curing with sea salt, and brief marination in rice vinegar, producing a translucent, slightly chewy delicacy with a subtle oceanic sweetness.

By the 17th century, engawa had become a luxury item traded across the Sanese archipelago. The Kofu Clan maintained strict quality controls, establishing a {{nihongo|'''engawa guild'''|縁側組|Engawa-gumi}} that regulated preparation standards and restricted the use of the Kofu name to products meeting their exacting criteria. This guild system, one of the earliest forms of geographical indication in Okaiken, ensured that Kofu engawa commanded premium prices in the markets of [[Okami]] and [[Mito]].

===The Oyster Renaissance===
The 19th century brought a revolutionary transformation to Kofu's maritime economy. While natural oyster reefs had been harvested for centuries, the [[Saito Restoration]]'s modernization policies enabled the systematic cultivation of oysters using techniques imported from the [[Kingdom of Zong]]. In 1884, the {{nihongo|'''Kofu Oyster Cooperative'''|甲府牡蠣組合|Kofu Kakigumi}} was established, introducing suspended raft cultivation in the sheltered waters of Kofu Bay.

The cooperative's innovations proved transformative. By suspending oyster spat from floating rafts in the bay's nutrient-rich currents, the cooperative achieved growth rates far exceeding natural reef production. The {{nihongo|'''Kofu Kaki'''|甲府牡蠣|Kofu Kaki}}—a plump, cream-colored oyster with a distinctive briny-sweet flavor and mineral finish—rapidly gained recognition as one of the finest oysters in the Shendan Ocean. The oyster industry's success spawned the {{nihongo|'''Oyster Renaissance'''|牡蠣ルネサンス|Kaki Runesansu}}, a period of unprecedented prosperity that saw Kofu's coastal waters transformed into a vast aquaculture landscape.

===Modern Development===
The completion of the [[Harujima Shinkansen]] through Kofu in 1967 integrated the prefecture into the high-speed transportation network, enabling same-day delivery of fresh oysters and engawa to metropolitan markets. The prefecture also developed significant citrus cultivation on the hillsides above the coastal plains, producing the {{nihongo|'''Kofu Mikan'''|甲府蜜柑}}, a sweet mandarin orange that complements the maritime economy.

Today, Kofu Prefecture maintains its position as Okaiken's premier oyster and engawa producer while diversifying into sustainable aquaculture, citrus agriculture, and coastal tourism centered on the scenic bay and its aquaculture heritage.

==Geography==
[[File:Kofu Bay Aquaculture.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Oyster cultivation rafts on the calm waters of Kofu Bay, with the coastal hills visible in the background.]]

Kofu Prefecture occupies a coastal region on the southern shore of Harujima Island, facing the Sea of Mitoyagi. The prefecture's defining geographic feature is {{nihongo|'''Kofu Bay'''|甲府湾|Kofu-wan}}, a large, sheltered inlet approximately 40 kilometers in width, protected by a chain of small islands and peninsulas that create calm, shallow waters ideal for aquaculture. The bay's unique geography—where warm currents from the Shendan Ocean meet the cooler waters of the Sea of Mitoyagi—produces the nutrient-rich conditions that support both the flatfish populations and the oyster beds.

The coastal plain is broader than in neighboring prefectures, extending up to 15 kilometers inland before rising into the {{nihongo|'''Kofu Hills'''|甲府丘陵|Kofu Kyūryō}}, gentle, south-facing slopes that provide the sun exposure and drainage essential for citrus cultivation. The {{nihongo|'''Hayakawa River'''|早川|Hayakawa-gawa}} is the prefecture's primary watercourse, flowing from the interior hills through the capital city into the bay.

The prefecture includes several small offshore islands within Kofu Bay, the most significant being {{nihongo|'''Okishima'''|沖島}} and {{nihongo|'''Nakajima'''|中島}}, which serve as bases for the aquaculture operations and maintain traditional fishing communities.

