==Economy==
[[File:Kofu Oyster Market.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The Kofu Morning Oyster Market, where the day's harvest is auctioned to restaurants and distributors.]]

Kofu Prefecture possesses a specialized maritime economy centered on engawa flounder fin production, oyster aquaculture, and citrus cultivation. The prefecture's sheltered bay and gentle hillsides create a unique combination of marine and agricultural resources that has defined its economic identity for centuries.

===Engawa and Seafood===
The {{nihongo|'''engawa'''|縁側}} fishery remains the cultural cornerstone of Kofu Prefecture's economy. The shallow waters of Kofu Bay support significant populations of {{nihongo|'''hirame'''|平目}} (olive flounder) and {{nihongo|'''karei'''|カレイ}} (dab flounder), whose fins are harvested for the prized delicacy. The Kofu engawa is distinguished by its exceptional thickness and translucency—qualities attributed to the bay's unique temperature profile, where the mixing of warm and cold currents stimulates the flatfish to develop particularly robust fin musculature.

The preparation of engawa follows strict traditional protocols established by the historical engawa guild. Master preparers, known as {{nihongo|'''engawa-shi'''|縁側師}}, undergo years of apprenticeship to master the precise knife work required to separate the fin from the flesh without damaging the delicate connective tissue. The finished product is served as sashimi, lightly seared as {{nihongo|'''aburi-engawa'''|炙り縁側}}, or incorporated into premium sushi preparations. Kofu engawa commands prices 3-4 times higher than products from other regions, and the prefecture's annual production of approximately 280 tonnes represents the bulk of Okaiken's high-end engawa supply.

Beyond engawa, the prefecture's fishing fleet harvests sea bream, squid, and various shellfish from the bay and surrounding waters. The {{nihongo|Kofu Morning Market|甲府朝市|Kofu Asaichi}}, held daily on the waterfront, is one of the most renowned seafood markets in southern Harujima.

===Oyster Aquaculture===
[[File:Kofu Oyster Raft.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Oyster cultivation rafts on Kofu Bay, showing the suspended rope culture method developed by the Kofu Oyster Cooperative.]]

Oyster aquaculture is Kofu Prefecture's most economically significant industry. The {{nihongo|'''Kofu Kaki'''|甲府牡蠣|Kofu Kaki}} is cultivated using the suspended raft method introduced by the Kofu Oyster Cooperative in 1884 and refined over more than a century of practice. The bay's sheltered waters, rich in phytoplankton carried by the converging currents, provide ideal growing conditions that produce oysters of exceptional size and flavor.

The Kofu Kaki is characterized by its deep, cup-shaped shell, plump cream-colored meat, and a distinctive flavor profile that balances briny intensity with a sweet, mineral finish. The oysters are harvested primarily during the winter months (November through March), when cold water temperatures produce the densest, most flavorful meat. Annual production exceeds 12,000 tonnes, making Kofu one of the largest oyster-producing prefectures in Okaiken.

The aquaculture industry has also spawned a thriving secondary economy in pearl cultivation. While not as large as the food oyster operations, several farms in the outer bay produce the {{nihongo|'''Kofu Pearl'''|甲府真珠|Kofu Shinju}}, a small, lustrous pearl prized for its warm golden overtone. The prefecture's pearl farms contribute to the luxury goods market and attract tourists to the aquaculture tours.

===Citrus Agriculture===
The hillsides above the coastal plain support extensive citrus cultivation. The {{nihongo|'''Kofu Mikan'''|甲府蜜柑}} is a sweet, easy-peeling mandarin orange that benefits from the south-facing slopes and the bay's moderating influence on temperature. The citrus industry complements the maritime economy, providing winter employment for aquaculture workers during the oyster off-season and creating a distinctive agricultural landscape of terraced groves rising above the bay.

===Traditional Crafts===
Kofu Prefecture maintains several traditional craft industries linked to its maritime heritage. The {{nihongo|'''Kofu Shima'''|甲府嶋}} is a distinctive textile woven from threads dyed with oyster shell extracts, producing a fabric with a subtle iridescent sheen. The prefecture is also known for its {{nihongo|'''kofu-yaki'''|甲府焼}}, a style of pottery featuring glazes made from the mineral-rich bay clays, which produces distinctive blue-green vessels traditionally used for serving oysters.
Kofu's economy combines high-value marine aquaculture with specialized precision tooling and fermentation. The prefecture's protected coastal coves support extensive cold-water flounder (engawa) and oyster farms, which supply fresh seafood to Okami's metropolitan markets. Inland machine shops produce stainless-steel valves, micro-filtration manifolds, and sterile bioreactor piping for Harujima's biotechnology facilities, while century-old mountain cellars produce aged cedar-cask miso and fermented koji starters that supply artisanal food manufacturers across western Okaiken.

==Transportation==
[[File:Kofu Station.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Kofu Station on the Harujima Shinkansen line, with the bay visible beyond the platform.]]

Kofu Prefecture maintains transportation connections reflecting its role as a maritime and agricultural center.

===Rail===
The prefecture is served by the [[Harujima Shinkansen]], with Kofu Station serving as a stop for both Nozomi and Nanpu services. The Shinkansen provides rapid access to Okami and the southern economic centers. Conventional [[Okaiken Railways]] services connect the prefecture's smaller cities to the regional rail network.

===Road===
The '''Kofu Expressway''' runs along the coast, connecting the prefecture to the Okami metropolitan expressway network. The highway is critical for transporting fresh oysters and engawa to metropolitan markets, with refrigerated trucks making multiple daily runs during peak season.

===Maritime===
The Port of Kofu serves as the primary harbor for the aquaculture industry, with dedicated facilities for oyster processing and shipment. Ferry services connect the bay islands to the mainland, while commercial vessels transport oyster products and seafood to destinations across the Shendan Ocean.

